# Packages this desktop needs.
#
# ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH. bin/null-package generates the RPM spec's Requires from
# this file, so the two cannot drift -- and they had: cava was here and not in
# the spec, so the ISO booted to a desktop whose spectrum said "cava did not
# start: No such file or directory". The spec's comment claimed a checker kept
# them in step. There was no checker. That is the same mistake as a comment
# describing a remap nothing performed.
#
# A package marked `# build-only` is needed to BUILD the tree and not to run
# it: the compiler, the bake's Python, the Vulkan tools the raytrace uses.
# Those become BuildRequires, never Requires -- an installed machine needs
# none of them, which is what shipping the hero prebuilt was for.

# Base packages for nox on Fedora.
#
# Per-distribution data (NULL.md §9.1): the names genuinely differ between
# distributions and no abstraction can fix that. Install with:
#     bin/pkg install-list base
#
# Every entry says why it is here. A package list without reasons is
# unreviewable a year later, which is when it matters.

# --- the font ----------------------------------------------------------
# The whole design rests on one bitmap typeface (NULL.md I2). The console
# build carries the PSF strikes the ramp is derived from and the virtual
# console uses; terminus-fonts serves the rest through fontconfig as .otb --
# which is BITMAP STRIKES IN AN OPENTYPE WRAPPER, not an outline build. It has
# strikes at 12,14,16,18,20,22,24,28,32 px and nothing in between.
#
# Measured, not assumed (verify/check-font-substitution.py): both WebKit and
# Gecko do render it -- so §8.10's "a bitmap font will not render in a browser
# engine" is not what happens here. What happens is worse to detect: they
# SCALE it. Widths come out exactly proportional to the requested size, so at
# any size that is not a strike the text is a scaled bitmap, which is the one
# thing this design exists to avoid (§9.3).
#
# Hence the TTF build, for contexts that cannot be pinned to a strike. Same
# design, same 4.49 series, main Fedora repository, OFL-1.1.
terminus-fonts                  # ter-u18n etc. (.otb strikes), via fontconfig
terminus-fonts-console          # ter-112n / ter-118n PSF, in /usr/lib/kbd/consolefonts/
ax86-terminus-ttf-fonts         # outline build, for browser engines (§8.10)

# --- the compositor ----------------------------------------------------
# Settled at Phase 0 against §8.1's six requirements. Hyprland is NOT
# packaged for Fedora and must not be used here -- it would come from a
# third-party repository, which §9.1 forbids for a core component.
sway
# THE THING THAT STARTS THE DESKTOP.
#
# Absent from this list, so a machine installed from the ISO had sway, had the
# whole desktop, and booted to a text console with no way to reach it: no
# display manager, and multi-user.target as the default. It went unnoticed
# because the build host already had sddm from before, and because every VM
# test started sway by hand over ssh -- neither is how anyone uses a machine.
#
# sddm specifically, because §9.7 and bin/null-system theme its greeter.
sddm

# THE SPLASH'S OWN MODULE.
#
# bake/make_boot_assets.py builds a theme whose ModuleName is two-step, and a
# plymouth theme naming a module that is not installed does not fall back --
# plymouth-set-default-theme refuses and the machine keeps whatever splash it
# had. Fedora ships the module separately from plymouth itself, and a minimal
# install has details, text and tribar and not this one.
#
# The same shape as sddm above: a surface this project builds, needing a
# package nothing asked for.
plymouth-plugin-two-step

# THE DISK HAS TO BOOT IN THE NEXT MACHINE.
#
# Fedora's dracut default is hostonly=yes, so the initramfs anaconda builds
# carries drivers for the storage the INSTALLING machine had. Measured on the
# test install: no sdhci, no mmc_block, no megaraid_sas, no i915, no amdgpu.
# That disk boots in the VM it was made in and drops to a dracut shell in a
# laptop with an SD-card reader or a RAID controller.
#
# This package is nine kilobytes of /usr/lib/dracut/dracut.conf.d saying
# hostonly="no". It is in the transaction, so the image anaconda builds is
# already generic; %post rebuilds and CHECKS anyway, because package ordering
# inside a transaction is not a guarantee. See `null-system initramfs`.
dracut-config-generic

# THE FIREWALL'S OWN BINARY, DECLARED RATHER THAN INHERITED.
#
# config/nftables/nulllinux.nft is this system's firewall and nftables.service
# loads it with /usr/sbin/nft. Nothing here asked for that binary. It arrived
# by:
#
#   @core -> firewalld -> python3-firewall -> python3-nftables -> nftables

# SOMETHING HAS TO ASK FOR THE PASSWORD.
#
# There was NO polkit authentication agent at all. polkit is what a desktop
# uses to ask "are you allowed to do that" for anything privileged -- mounting
# a USB stick through udisks2, changing a network setting through
# NetworkManager, installing a package -- and with no agent running there is
# nothing to put the question on screen. The call does not fail loudly; the
# prompt simply never appears and the action does not happen.
#
# polkit itself is here for the same reason nftables is: it was present only
# because something else dragged it in.
polkit

# xfce-polkit is the agent, chosen for two reasons and no others: it is 41 KiB
# and one binary at /usr/libexec/xfce-polkit, and it is GTK3 -- so the dialog
# resolves /usr/share/themes/nullLinux/gtk-3.0/gtk.css and arrives in this
# system's palette rather than in a toolkit default. The Qt agents would not:
# there is no Qt platform theme here, so lxqt-policykit and polkit-kde would
# render in stock Fusion light.
#
# It is not the eventual answer. A prompt that is genuinely of this system is
# text in Terminus hosted in the column, like every other component -- see
# NULL.md 8.4. This is the one that makes privileged actions WORK in v1, styled
# by inheritance rather than by construction.
xfce-polkit

# THE SOUND SERVER, DECLARED.
#
# Audio works on an installed machine -- verified: an ordinary user's session
# reaches PipeWire on /run/user/1000/pulse/native. It works because @core and
# the desktop stack drag pipewire and wireplumber in, not because anything here
# asked for them. Same shape as nftables and polkit: the thing this system
# depends on for a component NULL.md 8.4 lists by name is not in its own
# package list.
#
# wireplumber is the session manager. Without it pipewire runs and routes
# nothing, so every audio surface reports a failure that looks like missing
# hardware -- which is exactly how "audio has never worked" stayed in the
# status notes for weeks.
pipewire
wireplumber
pipewire-pulseaudio

# --- opening things ------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT WAS MISSING. bin/null-open knew three roles -- dir, reveal, editor --
# so a screenshot, a PDF, a video or a zip had no answer at all. grim is
# installed and bound to Print, which means this system could take a picture it
# had no way of showing you.
#
# Each of these is here for the design system as much as for the capability.
# The first three are keyboard-driven and take every colour from a config file,
# so config/ puts them in the palette instead of leaving them at a toolkit
# default. A GTK4/libadwaita viewer would ignore both the theme and the config.

imv                             # images. Wayland-native; -b and -t are palette
                                # roles, set in config/imv/config
zathura                         # PDF. Every colour it draws is a zathurarc
zathura-pdf-mupdf               # setting, which is what lets it be OF this
                                # system rather than merely dark
mpv                             # video and audio, one player for one verb
xarchiver                       # archives. GTK3, so it resolves
                                # /usr/share/themes/nullLinux/gtk-3.0/gtk.css
thunar-archive-plugin           # ... and the file manager can call it
tar                             # xarchiver is a FRONT END. It shells out, and
zip                             # its binary names `tar` 45 times and `zip` 40.
unzip                           # NONE of these six is in Fedora's @core -- I
gzip                            # checked rather than assumed, because "surely
xz                              # gzip is in the base system" is exactly the
bzip2                           # assumption that leaves a hole.
                                #
                                # tar and zip were absent outright, so "Extract
                                # here" on a .tar.gz -- the commonest archive on
                                # Linux -- and "Compress to .zip" both did
                                # nothing at all. The other four were present
                                # only because something else had dragged them
                                # in, which §9.1 calls what it is: a package a
                                # dependency change nobody made deliberately can
                                # remove.
                                #
                                # Found by asking a running guest what it had.
                                # An absence is invisible in this file.
file                            # null-open dispatches on MIME type, read from
                                # the bytes, not on a name a download can lose

# --- firmware, because a laptop with no wifi cannot fix itself ------------
#
# ONE FAMILY IS MISSING, AND IT IS THE ONE THIS MACHINE NEEDS.
#
# linux-firmware pulls in a wide set of split-out subpackages by itself --
# watching an install go past, it brought amd-gpu, amd-ucode, atheros,
# brcmfmac, cirrus-audio, intel-audio, intel-gpu, mt7xxx, nvidia-gpu,
# mrvlwireless, qcom-wan, realtek and tiwilink. It does NOT bring iwlwifi.
#
# An install here had 345 firmware files and ZERO iwlwifi ones -- so on a
# ThinkPad T480, which is the machine this is going onto and whose wlp3s0 is
# bound to iwlwifi, the card would come up and find no firmware to load.
#
# That is the worst shape a missing package can take: the thing you need to
# download the fix is the thing that is missing.
#
# Found by asking a running guest what it had. `ls /lib/firmware/iwlwifi* |
# wc -l` -> 0. Not visible from here, and not visible from the kickstart, which
# says @core + kernel + nulllinux and looks complete.
#
# THE WIFI FAMILIES, not every blob on earth. Intel is the one that was
# actually absent; Atheros, Realtek and Qualcomm arrive on their own today and
# are named anyway, because a package that is not declared is a package a
# dependency change nobody made deliberately can remove (§9.1) -- which is
# exactly what happened to iwlwifi.
iwlwifi-mvm-firmware            # Intel 7260..AX200 -- the T480's 8265, and most
                                # Intel wifi of the last decade
iwlwifi-dvm-firmware            # Intel 4965..6300, older ThinkPads
iwlwifi-mld-firmware            # Intel BE200 and later
atheros-firmware                # ath9k/ath10k/ath11k
realtek-firmware                # rtw88/rtw89, and Realtek bluetooth
qcom-firmware                   # Qualcomm, including many ARM laptops
amd-gpu-firmware                # an AMD GPU with no firmware does not modeset,
                                # which is a black screen rather than slow
                                # graphics

# --- the desktop's own plumbing ------------------------------------------
#
# THE FILE CHOOSER IS A WINDOW TOO. Without a portal backend, any application
# asking for "open a file" gets whatever the dependency graph happened to
# drag in -- or nothing, and the dialog never appears. The GTK backend is
# chosen because it is GTK3 and therefore inherits the theme; the wlr backend
# is what makes screen sharing work at all on a wlroots compositor.
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
gvfs-mtp                        # a phone plugged in over USB appears at all
bc                              # the calculator component (NULL.md 8.4) is a
                                # column topic over bc, not a fifth GUI

# AN EDITOR, DECLARED RATHER THAN INHERITED.
#
# null-open's editor role degrades through nvim, vim, vi and nano, and on the
# test install it landed on nano -- which is present because @core's
# dependencies happen to bring it, not because anything here asked. The same
# shape as nftables, polkit and pipewire: a role the system offers, resting on
# a package nobody declared.
#
# nano rather than neovim, deliberately. This desktop is terminal-first, so the
# editor inherits foot's palette and needs no theming of its own; neovim would
# be a configuration project in its own right and is a preference, not a
# default. Anyone who wants it installs it and null-open picks it up first.
nano

# --- printing ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There was no printing of any kind. GTK's print dialog talks to CUPS, so with
# no CUPS there is no dialog -- printing from any application simply offered
# nothing, which is not a state a general-purpose desktop should be in.
#
# system-config-printer is the management window: adding a printer, seeing the
# queue. GTK3, so it resolves the nullLinux theme and arrives in the palette
# rather than as a grey box.
cups
cups-filters
system-config-printer

# --- removable media, which had nothing behind it ------------------------
#
# thunar Requires NONE of these. Checked: `dnf repoquery --requires --resolve
# thunar` matches neither gvfs, nor udisks2, nor thunar-volman. So a USB stick
# plugged into an installed machine might mount, or might do nothing at all,
# depending on what else happened to be dragged in -- and "plug in a stick"
# is not an advanced operation, it is one of the first things anybody does.
#
# udisks2 is what mounts a device without asking for a root password. gvfs is
# what thunar uses for mounts, for network locations, and for TRASH: without
# it, deleting a file in the file manager is not a move to the trash, it is a
# delete. thunar-volman is what notices the device was plugged in at all.
udisks2
gvfs
thunar-volman

# AND THE FILESYSTEMS A STICK ACTUALLY HAS ON IT. A drive over 32 GB is
# formatted exFAT out of the box and anything from a Windows machine is NTFS.
# The kernel can read both; these are what let this system CHECK and FORMAT
# them, which is the half a desktop is expected to have.
exfatprogs
dosfstools
ntfs-3g

# --- installing an application that is not in Fedora ----------------------
#
# WITHOUT THIS, "install the app you want" ends at whatever Fedora packages.
# That is a real limit for a general-purpose desktop: no Spotify, no Discord,
# no Steam, nothing that ships only as a flatpak.
#
# flatpak ITSELF is in Fedora's own repositories, so it is an ordinary
# dependency. NO REMOTE IS ADDED. NULL.md 9.1: "Anything not in the
# distribution's own repositories is a separate, clearly marked tier, and must
# never be a hard dependency of a core component. Third-party repositories are
# a deliberate, recorded decision each time." Flathub is exactly that decision,
# and it is not mine to make on someone's behalf -- it is one command when they
# want it:
#
#   flatpak remote-add --if-not-exists flathub \
#     https://dl.flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo
#
# The portal backends this needs are already declared above; a flatpak
# application with no portal cannot open a file at all.
#
# A NOTE ON HOW THOSE APPLICATIONS WILL LOOK. They are sandboxed and do not see
# /usr/share/themes, so a flatpak arrives in its own colours until the theme is
# given to it. That is third-party application INTERIORS, which NULL.md 8.10
# treats as its own tier -- not a system window, and not claimed as styled here.
flatpak
#
# Four hops, every one through firewalld -- the package we deliberately stopped
# using. Remove firewalld as the obvious tidy-up after replacing it and `nft`
# goes with it: nftables.service fails at boot, and the %post fallback that
# would re-enable firewalld has nothing left to re-enable. The machine ends up
# with no firewall and nothing saying so.
#
# NULL.md 9.1: a package this project depends on is named here.
nftables

# --- GPU / bake --------------------------------------------------------
# The simulation is a Vulkan compute workload (§5.5). NVK exposes the
# discrete adapter, so no proprietary driver is required; this was verified
# at Phase 0 rather than assumed.
mesa-vulkan-drivers             # build-only
vulkan-tools                    # build-only -- vulkaninfo: confirms which adapters exist

# --- renderer build dependencies ---------------------------------------
# The renderer is a Wayland layer-shell client (§6.3). libxkbcommon is not
# optional even for surfaces that take no keyboard: the toolkit links it, and
# the column needs it for real (§7.3), since a keycode is not a character and
# only the layout says which.
rust                            # build-only
cargo                           # build-only
wayland-devel                   # build-only
libxkbcommon-devel              # build-only

# --- components (§8.4) --------------------------------------------------
# Each is named because a component needs it, not because it is nice to have.
fzf                 # the filter-picker every menu is (§7.4)
btop                # the monitor the column hosts (§10.5 decided this)
foot                # a terminal that renders bitmap fonts correctly
fd-find             # `find` re-runs this per keystroke; the picker's own matcher is off
brightnessctl       # the backlight, which settings reads and writes rather than owning
dunst               # the notification daemon the on-screen display uses (§7.5).
                    # Not mako: dunst already owns the bus name here, and a
                    # second daemon simply cannot take it.
grim slurp          # capture
wl-clipboard        # clipboard, and putting a capture's path where it can be pasted

# --- capture ------------------------------------------------------------
# §8.4: capture is both halves in one place -- stills and recordings.
# grim and slurp are already pulled in by the compositor spin.
wf-recorder                     # screen recording on wlroots

# --- the last three components (§8.4) ---------------------------------
# playerctl is already pulled in by the bar; these are the two that were not.
cliphist                        # clipboard history, for the clipboard component
cava                            # the audio spectrum in the column (§10.7 warns
                                # this dependency can multiply a surface's cost;
                                # it is measured in docs/measurements.md)

# The bake's Python dependencies. Absent from this list until a clean machine
# tried to run it: numpy for the simulation and the quantiser, pillow for the
# boot surfaces, gobject for the icon theme. They are on nox because something
# else pulled them in, which is exactly why they were never noticed missing.
# numpy IS BUILD-ONLY AGAIN, and this time the reason holds.
#
# It briefly was not: an installed machine derives its own grid from the master
# (bin/null-hero), the quantiser was numpy, and claiming build-only would have
# left a machine unable to draw its own wallpaper. The deriver is Rust now --
# it agrees with the Python to within one ramp step, checked by
# verify/check-derive-parity.sh, and runs in a tenth of the time -- so nothing
# on an installed machine needs numpy, and the 30 MB goes away again.
python3-numpy                   # build-only
python3-pillow                  # build-only
python3-gobject                 # build-only

# Found missing by installing onto a clean machine and running the suite there.
# Every one of these is on nox because something else pulled it in, and every
# one of them is invoked by a binding, a menu topic or a verification check --
# so on a fresh machine those failed silently or drew nothing.
thunar                          # the file manager (§8.7)
xfconf                          # xfconf-query, which sets thunar's defaults
firefox                         # named by a binding; the command check fails without it
git                             # the diff and status terminal surfaces (§7.6)
bluez                           # bluetoothctl, for the bluetooth menu topic

# THINGS THE DESKTOP OFFERS AND THE PACKAGE DID NOT INSTALL.
#
# Found by auditing a machine installed from the ISO rather than this build
# host: the settings menu has a `netconfig` topic that runs nmtui and a `mixer`
# topic that runs wiremix, and neither program was there. A menu entry that
# cannot work is worse than one that is absent -- it looks like a feature.
#
# The §10.1 check that every command exists runs against the SWAY BINDINGS, and
# these are reached through the menu, so it never saw them. And on this build
# host they happen to be installed, which is why nobody noticed.
NetworkManager-tui              # nmtui, the network topic in the settings menu
wiremix                         # the PipeWire mixer the column hosts (bin/null-mixer)
swaylock                        # the lock the idle ladder calls (§8.5)
swayidle                        # and the daemon that decides when
playerctl                       # the player topic and the column's now-playing
wlsunset                        # the night light
iw                              # wireless signal, where /proc/net/wireless is absent
fwupd                           # fwupdmgr, the firmware channel in the update report
webkit2gtk4.1                   # the browser engine the font probe measures (§8.10)
